WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Welcome to Grade 6 English, Unit 8: Entertainment and Media.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.040
Welcome to Unit 8: Entertainment and Media.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.700
In today's world, entertainment is everywhere.

4
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.700
We watch films and documentaries on
screens, listen to podcasts and radio shows,

5
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.720
follow stories through books and graphic
novels, and even create our own content.

6
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.100
But how much do we think about the media we consume?

7
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.440
How does a film tell a story?

8
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.240
What makes a documentary different from a drama?

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.500
Why do some commercials stay in our
minds long after we have seen them?

10
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.020
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.800
By the end of this unit, you will be able to read and
understand texts about entertainment and media, including film,

12
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.920
television, and storytelling traditions.

13
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.440
you will also use participle adjectives correctly,

14
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.260
choosing between the -ed form (how someone feels)
and the -ing form (what causes the feeling).

15
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:05.090
you will also form sentences in the past simple
passive to describe events in film and history (e.g.

16
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.820
"the documentary was filmed in mogadishu").

17
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.220
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

18
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:18.400
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
ritual, disciple, eternal, revival, pagan, Catholic.

19
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.040
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

20
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.400
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

21
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:36.370
The reading sequence includes Entertainment and Media:
source text 1 and Entertainment and Media: source text 2.

22
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:37.760
Read actively.

23
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.140
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

24
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.940
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

25
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.720
The first language focus is
Participle Adjectives (-ed and -ing).

26
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:59.760
What the rule means and why it matters Participle adjectives
are words that come from verbs and work as describing words

27
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:00.760
(adjectives).

28
00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.000
They help us add detail and emotion to our sentences.

29
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.620
Understanding the difference between the -ed form and the
-ing form is one of the most important grammar skills at this

30
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.020
level,

31
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.760
because getting it wrong can change
the meaning of your sentence completely.

32
00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:23.080
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

33
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.640
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Vocabulary Expert Interview.

34
00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.140
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

35
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.180
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

36
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.960
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

37
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.540
You are ready to begin.

38
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.780
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

39
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.400
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

40
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.140
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
